    <fr:title text="Wrong Definition of FollowLast">Wrong Definition of FollowLast</fr:title>
    <fr:taxon>Daily</fr:taxon>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <fr:p>Until now I had been using the wrong definition of follow last sets. Rather than what I intially had,</fr:p>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:date>
          <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
          <fr:month>1</fr:month>
          <fr:day>24</fr:day>
        </fr:date>
        <fr:addr>sss-0010</fr:addr>
        <fr:route>sss-0010.xml</fr:route>
        <fr:title text="FollowLast Set">FollowLast Set</fr:title>
        <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <fr:p>The followlast set (<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(\cdot )]]></fr:tex>) of a grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> are all the characters that may follow a word in the language of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> in a string that is in the language of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex>.

  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ \mathsf {FLast}(A) = \{ c ~|~ \exists  w , w_A . w_A \in  L(A) \land  w_Acw \in  L(A) \}]]></fr:tex></fr:p>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <fr:p>I should instead require <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[w_A]]></fr:tex> to be empty in
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ \mathsf {FLast}(A) = \{ c ~|~ \exists  w , w_A . w_A \in  L(A) \setminus  \{ \epsilon  \} \land  w_A c w \in  L(A) \}]]></fr:tex></fr:p>
    <fr:p>Or at least that's what they have in <fr:link href="krishnaswami_typed_2019.xml" title="A typed, algebraic approach to parsing" addr="krishnaswami_typed_2019" type="local">Neel's paper</fr:link>. Max believes I should have a more compelling argument for which definition is better/more appropriate, which is fair enough. The best argument I have thus far is that our original definition would break the property that
 <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(A \oplus  B) = \mathsf {FLast}(A) \cup  \mathsf {FLast}(B)]]></fr:tex></fr:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
